\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Per-subgroup correction requires $n_{\text{expert}}/K \geq 50$ expert labels per subgroup and moderate classifier accuracy ($\text{FPR}_s, \text{FNR}_s < 0.5$); at $K{=}8$, RMSE exceeds global correction under fixed expert budget (Appendix~\ref{app:k8_subgroups}).
Assumption~(A3) requires balanced effect modifier prevalence; under class imbalance ($P(Z{=}1) = 0.1$), the identity remains exact ($R^2 \geq 0.976$) but EC-HTE's RMSE advantage narrows (ratio 1.06--1.68; Appendix~\ref{app:classimbalance}).
Assumption~(A1)---conditional independence of misclassification and potential outcomes---is not empirically testable; sensitivity analysis shows robustness up to $\gamma_T \leq 0.05$ for treatment-dependent misclassification (coverage $\geq$89.7\%; Appendix~\ref{app:treatment_dep}).
Ground-truth subgroup CATEs are unavailable in public datasets---classic causal inference benchmarks lack the LLM annotation step that creates the measurement error we address---so all evaluations use synthetic CATE injection on real text features and real LLM annotations (Appendices~\ref{app:dgp},~\ref{app:multilm}).
Code and data generation scripts are publicly available.\footnote{\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/ec-hte}}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Consider estimating whether a content moderation policy reduces hate speech differently across demographic groups, using GPT-4 to classify thousands of posts.
After standard confusion-matrix correction, the analysis concludes the policy \emph{increases} hate speech among younger users---but the true effect is protective.
This sign reversal is not an edge case.

Large language models (LLMs) now routinely replace human annotators in computational social science, producing labels for sentiment, hate speech, stance, and political discourse at costs orders of magnitude below manual coding~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt,tornberg2024chatgpt,ziems2024nlp,tan2024llm}.
When these labels serve as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect (HTE) analysis, the standard correction protocol\footnote{Throughout, ``debiasing'' refers to correction of annotation measurement-error bias, not algorithmic fairness debiasing.} is straightforward: validate a sample against expert annotations, estimate a confusion matrix, and apply its inverse uniformly to correct conditional average treatment effect (CATE) estimates.
Yet LLM annotations are not uniformly accurate: \citet{ashwin2025bias} document error rates varying by up to 5$\times$ across demographic subgroups; in our pilot, GPT-4.1 error rates range from 10.2\% to 20.0\% across text-feature subgroups ($\Delta\pi = 0.098$).

We show that this standard correction can make things worse.
In 14.9\% of our synthetic configurations (across a grid of $\Delta\pi$ and $\tau(0)$ values; $\tau(0) = 0.03$), global correction reverses the sign of estimated CATE---a protective treatment ($\tau = +0.03$) appears harmful ($\hat{\tau} = -0.059$)---rising to 31\% at $\tau(0) = 0.01$ (Figure~\ref{fig:sign_reversal}); all eight real LLMs tested amplify bias under global correction, while our per-subgroup correction (EC-HTE; Section~\ref{sec:method}) eliminates sign reversal in the primary $K{=}2$ setting.
Global correction amplifies bias by up to 5.9$\times$ ($K{=}4$ with CausalForest); the classical Frazis--Loewenstein attenuation correction~\cite{frazis2003estimating} \emph{increases} bias by up to 2.9$\times$.
The mechanism is over-correction: pooled correction calibrated for the average error rate introduces substantial bias in low-error subgroups where the naive estimator is nearly unbiased.

We derive an exact bias identity explaining when and why (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}), verified at $R^2 \geq 0.97$ on synthetic grids.
The per-subgroup correction prescribed by the identity reduces $|\text{bias}|$ by $7.4\times$ under extreme heterogeneity; the advantage is conditional on sufficient $\Delta\pi$ and expert budget.

Existing corrections---prediction-powered inference (PPI)~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi}, design-based supervised learning (DSL)~\cite{egami2024dsl}, prediction-powered conditional inference (PPCI)~\cite{sui2026ppci}, arm-specific PPI~\cite{mozer2026ppi}, ForestIV~\cite{bu2023forestiv}---assume homogeneous prediction error; none addresses effect modifier misclassification (Section~\ref{sec:related}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_sign_reversal.pdf}
\caption{CATE sign reversal under global correction ($K{=}2$, $\tau(0) = 0.03$). (A) Naive vs.\ global-corrected CATE; red triangles mark sign reversals (7.5\% of subgroup estimates). (B) Extreme case: true $\tau = +0.03$, estimated $\hat{\tau} = -0.059$. (C) 14.9\% of configurations exhibit sign reversal; all 8 real LLMs amplify bias; EC-HTE: 0\% reversal.}
\label{fig:sign_reversal}
\end{figure*}

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item An exact bias identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}): global correction introduces subgroup-level bias $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau / (1-2\bar{\pi})$, explaining when and why it reverses CATE sign; verified at $R^2 \geq 0.97$ ($R^2 = 0.975$ across 11 real LLMs). The identity yields a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}) predicting when correction increases bias from error rates alone.
    \item Empirical operating guidelines: per-subgroup correction outperforms global correction when $\Delta\pi > 0.15$, $n_{\text{expert}}/K \geq 50$, and $K \leq 4$ subgroups; outside this envelope, the diagnostic guides practitioners to retain standard correction.
    \item One concrete instantiation: hierarchical Bayesian confusion matrix estimation (EC-HTE), verified for DIM, CausalForestDML, and R-learner, and composable with PPCI for joint covariate/outcome error.
\end{itemize}
Code and data generation scripts are publicly available.\footnote{\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/ec-hte}}

